  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT TUE OCT 6 2026\n\nCentral Alabama will be treated to a few more pleasant days as\nsurface high pressure and weak ridging aloft remain in control.\nDrier air in place will lead to cooler mornings with lows in the\nlow to mid 50s for most. Unfortunately, high temps look to climb\nup into the mid to upper 80s. I suppose this is the trade off we\nhave to make for now. \n\nThe main focus in this forecast period will be the progression of\nTropical Depression 9. TD9 was named earlier this afternoon \nacross the southwestern Gulf. It is currently forecast to quickly \nstrengthen into a Cat 2 hurricane by Friday afternoon over the \nnorthern Gulf, eventually making landfall late Friday into \nSaturday morning. There remains some uncertainty regarding how \nquickly this system turns to the northeast and its forward speed \nacross the Gulf. For now, users should continue to check back for \nthe latest forecast from us as well as our friends over at the \nNational Hurricane Center. As always keep in mind that impacts can\nand will likely be felt well outside of the forecast cone.\n\nNow, let's talk about impacts. For Central Alabama, the highest\nconfidence impact at this time is heavy rainfall and potential\nfor flash flooding. The next highest impact will be the potential\nfor windy conditions and possible downed trees/power outages. The\nlowest confidence impact will be the potential for tropical\ntornadoes. More details on these threats will be included below.\n\nHeavy Rainfall/Flash Flooding:\n\nDrought remains ongoing over the area, but some areas did receive\nbeneficial rainfall last weekend, especially our far southwest\nand far southeast counties. Streamflows remain below normal to\nwell below normal for this time of year across West Alabama,\nwhile streamflows are closer to normal for this time of year\n(though normal is fairly low this time of year) elsewhere. Still,\nwith copious tropical moisture transport we will be closely\nmonitoring the potential for flash flooding. It's too early to\nfocus on rainfall amounts quite yet, as they will be dependent on\nthe speed, strength, and track of the system, but WPC does have a\nhigher-end slight risk of excessive rainfall across all of\nAlabama on Saturday.\n\nGradient/Tropical Winds:\n\nThe magnitude of the wind threat will be highly dependent on the\nstrength and track of the system. One thing to keep an eye on is\nthe high pressure system across the northeast resulting in a cold\nair damming wedge. This will tighten up the pressure gradient and\ncause easterly winds to be quite breezy as the system approaches.\nThe latest LREF hints at a 60-75% chance for gusts greater than\n30 MPH on Saturday. At minimum, would expect at least a wind\nadvisory with this system and associated potential for downed\ntrees and power outages.\n\nSevere Weather/Tropical Tornadoes:\n\nUnsurprisingly, low-level shear and helicity values are high as\nthe tropical system moves in. Mid-level winds/deep-layer shear\nvalues are higher than normal for a tropical system due to\nphasing with the upstream shortwave trough. The main limiting\nfactor as it often is as we move into the cool season is\ninstability. The cold air damming wedge and associated easterly\nwinds resulting in evaporative cooling from isentropic lift\ndriven rainfall will be the primary limiting factor for surface-\nbased instability, unless pressure falls with the system are\nstrong enough. There's always a fine line between \"high shear low\nCAPE\" and \"high shear no CAPE\", so while the \"high shear no CAPE\"\nmay be the most likely scenario for most of the area, we will\nhave to keep an eye on anywhere some weak surface-based\ninstability can develop. Typically the northeast quadrant is the\nfavored quadrant, though will still need to keep an eye on the\nsoutheast quadrant later on in the event as the system becomes\nextratropical.\n\nTD9 quickly peels off to the northeast by early next week as\nupper level ridging and surface high pressure builds into the\nregion. This will promote a likely much needed drying out period\nearly next week.\n\n95/Castillo",
